ffiliation: School of Informatics, University of Edinburgh, UK    Lynne Baillie Affiliation: Dept.\nL43: of Mathematical and Computer Sciences, Heriot-Watt University, UK cb54@hw.ac.uk, l.baillie@hw.ac.uk, m.romeo@hw.ac.uk    Susan Shenkin Affiliation: Ageing and Health, and Advanced Care Research Centre, Usher Institute, University of Edinburgh, UK susan.shenkin@ed.ac.uk    Marta Romeo ^{†}^{†}thanks: This work was funded by the UK Engineering and Physical Sciences Research Council (Grant Number: EP/S023208/1) Affiliation: Dept.\nL44: of Mathematical and Computer Sciences, Heriot-Watt University, UK cb54@hw.ac.uk, l.baillie@hw.ac.uk, m.romeo@hw.ac.uk\nL45: ###### Abstract\nL46: Social robots offer a promising solution for autonomously guiding patients through physiotherapy exercise sessions, but effective deployment requires advanced decision-making to adapt to patient needs. A key challenge is the scarcity of patient behavior data for developing robust policies.\nL47: To address this, we engaged 33 expert healthcare practitioners as patient proxies, using their interactions with our robot to inform a patient behavior model capable of generating exercise performance metrics and subjective scores on perceived exertion.\nL48: We trained a reinforcement learning-based policy in simulation, demonstrating that it can adapt exercise instructions to individual exertion tolerances and fluctuating performance, while also being applicable to patients at different recovery stages with varying exercise plans.\nL49: ## I INTRODUCTION\nL50: Adherence to physical exercise programs is crucial for rehabilitating individuals who have sustained injuries such as fractures or strokes, significantly improving physical outcomes while delivering broader social and economic benefits [【29†1】]. However, healthcare workforce shortages in many countries mean patients can go weeks or months without in-person physiotherapy assessments, meaning they often rely on generic paper or video-based exercise guides [【30†2】].\nL51: While recent technological advances in the physiotherapy space show promise, many solutions remain reliant on constant human oversight, creating an increase in staff workload [【31†3】] and training [【32†4】], highlighting the need for autonomous, accessible approaches that can provide personalized rehabilitation support in community settings.\nL52: Social robots have demonstrated the potential to autonomously deliver exercise sessions [【33†5】, 【34†6】], adapting instructions based on real-time user feedback while leveraging their physical presence to enhance engagement beyond screen-based agents [【35†7】]. However, developing autonomous control policies is challenging, as patients differ in physical ability, with performance fluctuating daily, requiring the system to recognize and adapt to both good and bad days.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFrom 3D Pose to Prose: Biomechanics-Grounded Vision–Language Coaching (https://arxiv.org/html/2603.26938v1)\nciteturn8view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2603.26938v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 638\nL0: ##### Report GitHub Issue\nL1: \nL2: [Button: ×]\nL3: \nL4: Title: [Input: Enter title]\nL5: \nL6: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below:\nL7: \nL8: Description:\nL9: \nL10: [Button: Submit without GitHub] [Button: Submit in GitHub]\nL11: \nL12: 【0†Why HTML?†info.arxiv.org】 Report Issue 【1†Back to Abstract】 【2†Download PDF】 Contents Reading mode Colour theme\nL13:   1. 【3†Abstract】\nL14:   2. 【4†1 Introduction】\nL15:   3. 【5†2 Related Work】\nL16:   4. 【6†3 Method】\nL17:     1. 【7†3.1 Overview】\nL18:     2. 【8†3.2 Dual-Modality Feature Extraction】\nL19:     3. 【9†3.3 Exercise-Specific Degree-of-Freedom Selection】\nL20:     4. 【10†3.4 Structured Biomechanical Context Generation】\nL21:       1. 【11†3.4.1 Individual Morphometric Context Module】\nL22:       2. 【12†3.4.2 Motion Quality Context Module】\nL23:     5. 【13†3.5 Vision-Biomechanics Conditioned Feedback Generation】\nL24:     6. 【14†3.6 Training Objective】\nL25:     7. 【15†3.7 QEVD-bio-fit-coach Dataset】\nL26:     8. 【16†3.8 Implementation Details】\nL27:   5. 【17†4 Experiments】\nL28:     1. 【18†4.1 Experimental Setup】\nL29:     2. 【19†4.2 Results on QEVD-bio-fit-coach】\nL30:     3. 【20†4.3 Ablation Study】\nL31:     4. 【21†4.4 Limitations and Future Work】\nL32:   6. 【22†5 Conclusion】\nL33:   7. 【23†References】\nL34:   8. 【24†6 QEVD-bio-fit-coach Dataset Construction】\nL35:     1. 【25†6.1 Automated Generation Pipeline】\nL36:     2. 【26†6.2 Dataset Statistics】\nL37:   9. 【27†7 Evaluation Metrics】\nL38:   10. 【28†8 Exercise-Specific DoF Annotation Protocol】\nL39:   11. 【29†9 Static vs. Dynamic Joint Classification】\nL40:   12. 【30†10 Cycle Detection Algorithm Details】\nL41:   13. 【31†11 Implementation Details】\nL42:     1. 【32†11.1 DoF Selection Network Architecture】\nL43:     2. 【33†11.2 Visual Backbone Configuration】\nL44:     3. 【34†11.3 3D Skeletal Extraction and Morphometric Processing】\nL45:     4. 【35†11.4 Training Configuration】\nL46:   14. 【36†12 Mathematical Derivations】\nL47:     1. 【37†12.1 Vision-Morphometric Cross-Attention Details】\nL48:     2. 【38†12.2 Motion Context Serialization and Prompting】\nL49: 【39†License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license†info.arxiv.org】\nL50: \nL51: arXiv:2603.26938v1 [cs.CV] 27 Mar 2026\nL52: # From 3D Pose to Prose: Biomechanics-Grounded Vision–Language Coaching\nL53: \nL54: Yuyang Ji Affiliation:  Department of Computer Science, Drexel University    Yixuan Shen Affiliation:  Department of Computer Science, Drexel University    Shengjie Zhu Affiliation:  Department of Computer Science and Engineering, Michigan State University    Yu Kong Affiliation:  Department of Computer Science and Engineering, Michigan State University    Feng Liu Affiliation:  Department of Computer Science, Drexel University\nL55: ###### Abstract\nL56: We present BioCoach, a biomechanics-grounded vision–language framework for fitness coaching from streaming video.\nL57: BioCoach fuses visual appearance and 3D skeletal kinematics, through a novel three-stage pipeline: an exercise-specific degree-of-freedom selector that focuses analysis on salient joints; a structured biomechanical context that pairs individualized morphometrics with cycle and constraint analysis; and a vision–biomechanics conditioned feedback module that applies cross-attention to generate precise, actionable text.\nL58: Using parameter-efficient training that freezes the vision and language backbones, BioCoach yields transparent, personalized reasoning rather than pattern matching. To enable learning and fair evaluation, we augment QEVD-fit-coach with biomechanics-oriented feedback to create QEVD-bio-fit-coach, and we introduce a biomechanics-aware LLM judge metric.\nL59: BioCoach delivers clear gains on QEVD-bio-fit-coach across lexical and judgment metrics while maintaining temporal triggering; on the original QEVD-fit-coach, it improves text quality and correctness with near-parity timing, demonstrating that explicit kinematics and constraints are key to accurate, phase-aware coaching. 【40†Project†vilab-group.com】\nL60: ^{†}^{†}footnotetext: 🖂 fl397@drexel.edu\nL61: ## 1 Introduction\nL62: Live fitness coaching through streaming video is increasingly important, with a broad impact on at-home workouts [【41†44】], injury prevention [【42†31】, 【43†15】], and data-driven rehabilitation [【44†38】, 【45†1】, 【46†5】]. Traditionally, fitness coaching has relied on in-person trainers who provide immediate feedback to correct form deviations and reduce risks; however, expert coaching is expensive and often inaccessible.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n1 Introduction (https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16422v1)\nciteturn8view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16422v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 503\nL0: ##### Report GitHub Issue\nL1: \nL2: [Button: ×]\nL3: \nL4: Title: [Input: Enter title]\nL5: \nL6: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below:\nL7: \nL8: Description:\nL9: \nL10: [Button: Submit without GitHub] [Button: Submit in GitHub]\nL11: \nL12: 【0†Why HTML?†info.arxiv.org】 Report Issue 【1†Back to Abstract】 【2†Download PDF】 Contents Reading mode Colour theme\nL13:   1. 【3†1 Introduction】\nL14:     1. 【4†The fit phase is limited by the update rule】\nL15:     2. 【5†The tail phase is limited by where the simulator is spent】\nL16:     3. 【6†The iteration is limited by the host】\nL17:   2. 【7†2 Related Work】\nL18:     1. 【8†Humanoid motion tracking】\nL19:     2. 【9†Differentiable simulation and model-based gradients】\nL20:     3. 【10†Optimizers, batch size and step size】\nL21:     4. 【11†Allocation and evaluation】\nL22:   3. 【12†3 Problem Setup】\nL23:     1. 【13†Task】\nL24:     2. 【14†Evaluation】\nL25:     3. 【15†Training loop】\nL26:   4. 【16†4 BlinkMimic】\nL27:     1. 【17†4.1 Muon for the policy and the world model】\nL28:     2. 【18†4.2 A step size that starts high and falls on progress】\nL29:     3. 【19†4.3 A residual policy】\nL30:     4. 【20†4.4 A world model with the simulator’s structure】\nL31:     5. 【21†4.5 Spending the simulator on deployment failures】\nL32:     6. 【22†4.6 Making each iteration cheap】\nL33:       1. 【23†Imagination batch】\nL34:       2. 【24†Removing the host from the loop】\nL35:   5. 【25†5 Experiments】\nL36:     1. 【26†Protocol】\nL37:     2. 【27†5.1 Main result】\nL38:     3. 【28†5.2 What each ingredient contributes】\nL39:       1. 【29†Fit phase】\nL40:       2. 【30†Return level】\nL41:       3. 【31†Tail phase】\nL42:       4. 【32†Iteration cost】\nL43:     4. 【33†5.3 Why the fit phase is limited by the update rule】\nL44:       1. 【34†The imagined gradient is accurate where the policy acts】\nL45:       2. 【35†A perfect teacher is not faster】\nL46:       3. 【